---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PRACTICE, SITE } from '../consts';
---

<Base
  title="How we work"
  description="TheaterThink is our rehearsal-based practice: direct the work, cast the ensemble, rehearse before the stakes are irreversible. Three moves, in order."
  path="/how-we-work/"
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">The method</p>
    <h1>Direct. Cast. Rehearse.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most AI rollouts get staged like a product launch. Pick the tool, announce it, train
      people on the buttons, wait for adoption. Theater does not work that way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap page-body">
    <p>
      A director does not hand out the script and hope. The work is three moves, and they
      happen in order. We call the practice TheaterThink<sup>®</sup>—the applied method
      behind everything on this site.
    </p>

    {
      PRACTICE.map((step, i) => (
        <div class="section-rule">
          <p class="eyebrow">Move {i + 1}</p>
          <h2 style="margin-top:0">
            {step.name}: {step.subtitle}
          </h2>
          <p class="pull">{step.oneLiner}</p>
          <p>
            <strong>The core move.</strong> {step.move}
          </p>
        </div>
      ))
    }

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>Why rehearsal is the move everyone skips</h2>
      <p>
        Organizations buy training and call it rehearsal. They are not the same thing.
        Training is being told. Rehearsal is trying, failing in a room where failure is
        free, and adjusting before it matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        A bounded experiment makes assumptions visible. It produces evidence instead of
        opinion, and it lets a team disagree about something real. That is why our
        engagements are built as a series of attempts rather than a single plan handed
        over at the end.
      </p>
      <p class="pull">Rehearsal is a way of knowing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>Where AI fits</h2>
      <p>
        The work is not anti-AI. It asks people to become better directors of it. Every
        response a model gives is generated through a probabilistic process; fluency is
        not the same as truth. So we treat important outputs as material to evaluate, not
        authority to obey.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is a directing problem before it is a tooling problem. Prompting is rewriting
        the lines. Directing changes what the performer wants.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>The idea underneath</h2>
      <p>
        TheaterThink is the practice. The larger capacity it develops is called Artistic
        Intelligence: the ability to interpret what matters, compose with what is
        available, and discover direction through rehearsal. It is the subject of Josh's
        forthcoming book, and it is bigger than AI—AI is what makes it urgent right now.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Talk about your work</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={SITE.founderSite}>Artistic Intelligence, the book</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
